{"level":30,"time":1778188306333,"pid":122,"hostname":"e70bc6df-98e","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QAJJWn53ruxtzR2q5R9rGs","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Site Feature Clearances from Overhead Electric Lines\n     2→\n     3→## Description\n     4→\n     5→This grouping covers clearance requirements between overhead electric distribution and transmission facilities (poles, conductors, guy wires) and proposed site features. Reviewers verify that buildings, fences, retaining walls, driveways, parking areas, sidewalks, lighting, utilities, and other site improvements maintain required horizontal and vertical clearances from existing or relocated overhead electric infrastructure.\n     6→\n     7→## Regulatory Overview\n     8→\n     9→Austin Energy's clearance requirements establish two simultaneous clearance envelopes that must both be satisfied: (1) a **7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance** that extends infinitely upward from ground level, creating a vertical wall at 7.5 feet from the conductor, and (2) a **15-foot radial clearance** measured in all directions from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors, creating a cylindrical zone around the conductor. Both requirements apply to all customer facilities—buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, and other installations. The code explicitly prohibits installing customer facilities under or over Austin Energy overhead distribution facilities, separate from maintaining clearance distances.\n    10→\n    11→**Distribution vs. Transmission Facilities:**\n    12→The distinction between distribution and transmission facilities is critical because they have different clearance and vegetation requirements. Distribution facilities operate at voltages below 60kV phase-to-phase (typically 12.47kV or 34.5kV in Austin), while transmission facilities operate at 60kV or higher (69kV, 138kV, or 345kV in Austin). Transmission facilities are physically larger—taller poles, wider structures, greater conductor spacing—and require more restrictive clearances. Development within 100 feet of transmission lines triggers specialized engineering analysis by Austin Energy transmission staff. Transmission facilities cannot be de-energized or easily relocated for private development, unlike distribution facilities.\n    13→\n    14→**Conductor Types:**\n    15→UCM distinguishes between primary conductors (higher voltage distribution lines, typically 12.5kV), secondary conductors (lower voltage service lines), neutral conductors (grounded conductors in the distribution system), and service-drop conductors (overhead secondary voltage service from Austin Energy infrastructure to customer service point). The 15-foot radial clearance applies to overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors; the 7.5-foot sky-to-ground clearance applies to primary, neutral, and secondary conductors.\n    16→\n    17→**Key Thresholds:**\n    18→- **7.5 feet horizontal sky-to-ground clearance** from overhead primary, neutral, and secondary conductors (UCM § 1.10.3)\n    19→- **15-foot radius clearance** from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors, measured in all directions (UCM § 1.10.3)\n    20→- **16 feet minimum vertical clearance** for driveways and parking without truck traffic (UCM § 1.10.6.2)\n    21→- **18 feet minimum vertical clearance** for roads, streets, alleys, and areas with truck traffic (UCM § 1.10.6.2)\n    22→- **10 lateral feet** from overhead distribution conductors for tree placement (ECM § 2.4.1.D, § 2.4.2.C)\n    23→- **30-40 lateral feet** from overhead transmission conductors for tree placement (ECM § 2.4.1.D, § 2.4.2.C; UCM § 1.10.10.4 requires 50 lateral feet as more restrictive standard)\n    24→- **10-foot radial clearance** for construction working clearances from energized overhead lines (OSHA 1910.333, UCM § 1.10.2)\n    25→\n    26→**Measurement Methodology (Critical):**\n    27→All clearances must be measured from the **outside conductor** (the actual electric wire), never from the utility pole centerline or pole structure. On multi-conductor installations, the outermost conductor may be several feet offset from pole center. Measuring from pole centerline instead of the conductor results in understated clearances and code violations. Profile plans must show dimension lines originating at the conductor location, not at the pole.\n    28→\n    29→**Exceptions:**\n    30→- **Utility-compatible trees** (species marked \"UC\" in ECM Appendix F, generally mature height ≤20 feet) may be planted within 15 lateral feet of overhead distribution conductors where other trees cannot\n    31→- **Service-drop roof clearances**: 18-inch minimum clearance above roof if service mast is within 4 feet of roof edge (instead of standard 3-foot clearance) for voltages ≤300V\n    32→- **Transmission facility variations**: Austin Energy engineering staff may determine site-specific clearances for development within 100 feet of transmission lines using specialized analysis software and LiDAR survey data\n    33→\n    34→**Documentation Requirements:**\n    35→- **Proximity surveys**: As required by Austin Energy Design, customer must provide survey showing proximities of existing and proposed facilities to existing Austin Energy primary voltage facilities (UCM § 1.10.3)\n    36→- **Profile plans**: Engineered, sealed profile drawings showing vertical relationships between overhead conductors and building features, including overhangs, balconies, and canopies, with dimensioned clearances\n    37→- **48-hour notification plan note**: Plans must include note that owner/contractor must notify Austin Energy at least 48 hours before work begins near high-voltage overhead lines (Texas Health & Safety Code Chapter 752, Section 752.003)\n    38→\n    39→**Common Violation Patterns:**\n    40→- **Clearances measured from pole instead of conductor**: Dimension annotations read \"10'-0\" from pole\" instead of \"15'-0\" from outside conductor\"; conductor may be several feet offset from pole center on three-phase installations\n    41→- **Missing profile plans**: Site plan shows building and overhead lines in plan view only, no elevation/section view; no surveyed mid-span clearance data; cannot verify vertical clearances meet requirements\n    42→- **Facilities installed under/over overhead distribution**: Building footprint, canopy, sign, or structure shown directly beneath overhead conductor routing in violation of absolute prohibition regardless of clearance distances\n    43→- **Non-utility-compatible trees beneath lines**: Landscape plan shows large trees beneath or within 10 feet of overhead lines; species not from Utility Compatible list in ECM Appendix F\n    44→- **Inadequate service-drop clearances**: Driveway grade shown within 15-16 feet of overhead service drop without mid-span clearance verification; service drop sag at maximum load/temperature may contact vehicles\n    45→- **Missing 48-hour notification note**: Site has overhead lines and construction staging area adjacent, but no plan notes addressing notification requirement\n    46→- **Down guy conflicts**: Down guy anchors and guy wires omitted from plans; proposed accessible paths, driveways, or buildings shown on top of existing down guys\n    47→\n    48→## Code References\n    49→\n    50→- UCM § 1.10.0 - Clearance and Safety Requirements\n    51→- UCM § 1.10.2 - Minimum Working Clearances From Energized Overhead Electric Utility Lines (OSHA)\n    52→- UCM § 1.10.3 - Permanent Clearances From AE Overhead Distribution Lines and Facilities\n    53→- UCM § 1.10.4 - Clearances From AE Padmount Equipment and Distribution Vaults\n    54→- UCM § 1.10.6.2 - Service-Drop Conductor Clearances\n    55→- UCM § 1.10.9 - Clearances for Metering Equipment and Enclosures\n    56→- UCM § 1.9.2 - Clearances and Mounting Heights for Metering Equipment\n    57→- UCM § 1.9.3.1 - Location of Meter Sockets\n    58→- UCM § 1.14.0 - Transmission and Substation Requirements\n    59→- UCM § 1.17.0 - Glossary (definitions of Distribution, Transmission, Infrastructure, Conductors)\n    60→- ECM § 2.4.1.D - Street Yard Trees (tree placement near overhead conductors)\n    61→- ECM § 2.4.2.C - Trees in Parking Lots (tree placement near overhead conductors)\n    62→- ECM Appendix F - Descriptive Categories of Tree Species (Utility Compatible trees list)\n    63→- NESC Section 234 - Horizontal sky-to-ground and radial clearances from overhead electric conductors (incorporated by UCM § 1.10.3)\n    64→\n    65→## Documents to Review\n    66→\n    67→- **Site Plan Sheets** - Existing overhead electric facilities (poles, conductors, guy wires, easements) and all proposed site features with clearance dimensions labeled\n    68→- **Building Elevation Sheets** - Surveyed elevations of overhead conductors with clearance dimensions to building faces and projections\n    69→- **Section/Profile Exhibits** - Cross-sections showing clearance dimensions from outer conductor to site features (buildings, walls, fences, driveways)\n    70→- **Survey Data** - Registered surveyor data for pole locations, conductor elevations, mid-span clearances when vertical clearance verification needed\n    71→- **Landscape Plans** - Site grading near overhead transmission facilities and proposed landscaping clearances; tree species verification against ECM Appendix F Utility Compatible list\n    72→- **Utility Plans** - Existing electric pole locations, guy wires, conductor routing, easement boundaries with clearance annotations\n    73→- **Cover Sheet** - Notes regarding property owner responsibility for maintaining clearances per NESC, OSHA, and UCM; 48-hour notification requirement to Austin Energy before work near high-voltage lines\n    74→\n    75→## Checklist Items\n    76→\n    77→| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n    78→|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n    79→| EL-1.1 | Buildings and permanent structures do not maintain minimum 7 feet 6 inches horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from overhead electric conductors | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when buildings or permanent structures are proposed and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan for overhead poles/conductors and building locations; verify clearance measured from outside conductor, not pole. |\n    80→| EL-1.2 | Customer facilities do not maintain minimum 15-foot radial clearance from outside conductor of overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when customer facilities/installations of any type (buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, or other installations) are proposed and overhead distribution facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan and utility plan for overhead distribution lines (voltage <60kV, typically 12.47kV or 34.5kV in Austin) and facility setbacks; verify conductor type labeled as primary or neutral. |\n    81→| EL-1.3 | Building projections (overhangs, awnings, balconies, decks, roofs, patios) do not maintain required clearances from overhead electric conductors | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when building projections are shown and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Building elevations and section views for projections extending toward overhead conductors; verify both 7.5-foot sky-to-ground and 15-foot radial clearances from outside conductor. |\n    82→| EL-1.4 | Gas pump canopies do not maintain required clearances from overhead electric lines | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when gas pump canopies are proposed and overhead electric lines exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan for gas pump canopy locations and overhead line routing; verify both 7.5-foot sky-to-ground and 15-foot radial clearances. |\n    83→| EL-1.5 | Stairs, elevated walkways, and elevated platforms do not maintain minimum 15-foot radial clearance from overhead electric distribution facilities | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when stairs, elevated walkways, or elevated platforms are proposed and overhead electric distribution facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan and section views for elevated features near overhead lines; verify 15-foot radial clearance from outside conductor. |\n    84→| EL-1.6 | Buildings do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance and 15-foot radial clearance from overhead electric facilities in right-of-way adjacent to site | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when buildings are proposed and electric facilities exist or will be relocated in ROW adjacent to site. Check: Site plan for building setback from ROW and utility plan for electric facility locations or relocation notes; verify clearances are permanent requirements per UCM § 1.10.3, distinct from construction working clearances. |\n    85→| EL-1.7 | Surveyed elevations and locations of overhead conductors not included on building elevation sheets to demonstrate required clearances are met | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when buildings or elevated structures are proposed within potential clearance distance of overhead electric facilities. Check: Building elevation sheets for surveyed conductor elevation data and clearance dimension annotations; verify survey performed by registered surveyor. |\n    86→| EL-1.8 | Section and plan view exhibits do not show dimensions from outer electric conductor to outermost edge of buildings and building projections for clearance verification | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when buildings, permanent structures, or building projections are proposed within potential clearance distance of overhead electric facilities. Check: Section views and enlarged plan details for dimension lines showing clearances from outer conductor to building edges. |\n    87→| EL-1.9 | Fences do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from outside conductor of overhead electric facilities | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when fences are proposed and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan for fence alignments and dimension annotations showing clearance from outside conductor (not pole). |\n    88→| EL-1.10 | Fence posts do not maintain minimum 10-foot horizontal clearance from overhead electric poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when fences are proposed and overhead electric poles exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan for fence post locations relative to electric pole locations. |\n    89→| EL-1.11 | Fences connecting to or near substation fences not electrically isolated with 10-foot non-conductive isolation panel connecting the two fences | UCM § 1.14.0 | Only when fences are proposed adjacent to or connecting with existing Austin Energy substation fences. Check: Site plan for fence alignments near substation property and detail sheets for 10-foot non-conductive (wooden) panel specifications between metal fence and substation fence. |\n    90→| EL-1.12 | Fencing near electric transmission lines not constructed of non-conductive material or not properly grounded, or specifications and grounding details not provided | UCM § 1.14.0 | Only when fencing is proposed beneath or adjacent to electric transmission lines (voltage ≥60kV). Check: Site plan for transmission line proximity to fences (facilities labeled 69kV, 138kV, or 345kV, or within transmission easement) and detail sheets for fence material specifications and grounding detail drawings. |\n    91→| EL-1.13 | Vehicle barrier fencing beneath overhead electric lines does not maintain minimum 7.5-foot sky-to-ground clearance from outermost electric line or lacks vertical and horizontal clearance verification exhibits | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when vehicle barrier posts or fencing are proposed beneath or adjacent to overhead electric lines. Check: Site plan for barrier locations beneath overhead lines and section exhibits showing clearance dimensions from outermost conductor. |\n    92→| EL-1.14 | Retaining walls do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from outermost electric line where overhead facilities are present | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when retaining walls are proposed and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan and grading plan for retaining wall alignments and clearance dimensions from outermost electric line (measured from conductor, not pole). |\n    93→| EL-1.15 | Retaining walls adjacent to Austin Energy substation walls do not maintain minimum 5-foot separation to allow for grounding grid extension, wall inspection, wall replacement, and proper drainage | Reviewer Convention | Only when retaining walls are proposed adjacent to Austin Energy substation property. Check: Site plan for retaining wall locations within 10 feet of substation boundary and clearance dimension from substation wall. |\n    94→| EL-1.16 | Emergency access structures (bridges, elevated platforms) do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot sky-to-ground clearance and 15-foot radial clearance from outside conductor of overhead electric facilities | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when emergency access structures such as bridges or elevated platforms are proposed and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Section views showing emergency access structure elevations relative to overhead conductor elevations; verify both clearance envelopes satisfied. |\n    95→| EL-1.17 | Retaining walls lack section exhibits showing dimensions from outer electric conductor to outermost edge of wall for clearance verification | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when retaining walls are proposed within potential clearance distance of overhead electric facilities. Check: Section views for retaining wall clearance dimension annotations from outer conductor to wall face. |\n    96→| EL-1.18 | Driveways do not maintain minimum 16-foot vertical clearance (parking without truck traffic) or 18-foot vertical clearance (areas with truck traffic) from lowest point of overhead electric facilities including telecommunications attachments | UCM § 1.10.6.2 | Only when driveways are proposed or modified and overhead electric facilities cross over or are adjacent to driveway locations. Check: Grading plan and section views showing finished driveway grade elevations and overhead line clearance dimensions; verify survey of mid-span clearances if needed; determine whether truck traffic expected to establish 16-foot vs. 18-foot requirement. |\n    97→| EL-1.19 | Vehicles parked or displayed on concrete pads do not maintain minimum vertical clearance from lowest overhead electric lines, or requirement not noted on site plans | UCM § 1.10.6.2 | Only when vehicle parking or display areas on concrete pads are proposed beneath overhead electric lines. Check: Site plan and section views for parking pad locations beneath overhead lines, vertical clearance dimensions (12 feet for residential pedestrian areas, 16 feet for parking without trucks, 18 feet with truck traffic), and note about clearance requirement. |\n    98→| EL-1.20 | Final road and driveway grades do not maintain minimum 30-foot vertical clearance from electric transmission lines to ground surface | UCM § 1.14.0 | Only when roads or driveways are proposed or regraded beneath electric transmission lines (voltage ≥60kV; facilities labeled 69kV, 138kV, or 345kV). Check: Grading plan and utility plan for transmission line locations (check voltage labels, transmission easement designations, large-scale structures) and final grade elevations with vertical clearance dimensions. |\n    99→| EL-1.21 | Final highway and freeway grades with distribution underbuilt do not maintain minimum 40-foot vertical clearance from overhead electric lines to road surface | UCM § 1.14.0 | Only when highway or freeway grading is shown with existing distribution lines underbuilt on transmission structures. Check: Grading plan and cross-sections for roadway grades beneath transmission structures showing distribution underbuilt and 40-foot vertical clearance. |\n   100→| EL-1.22 | Existing electric poles in conflict with proposed driveways not relocated minimum 30 feet from edge of driveway | Reviewer Convention | Only when proposed driveways are shown in conflict with or immediately adjacent to existing electric pole locations. Check: Site plan for driveway alignments within 30 feet of existing poles; verify relocation notes or revised pole locations. |\n   101→| EL-1.23 | Clearance dimensions measured from utility pole or centerline instead of from outside conductor or outermost electric line as required by code | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when clearance dimensions are shown on plans and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Plan dimension annotations showing clearances—verify dimension lines extend from outside conductor (not pole center or centerline of three-phase lines); on multi-conductor installations, conductor may be several feet offset from pole center. |\n   102→| EL-1.24 | Streetlight poles located under overhead electric lines | Reviewer Convention | Only when new streetlight poles are proposed and overhead electric lines exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan and photometric plan for streetlight pole locations beneath overhead line routing. |\n   103→| EL-1.25 | Sidewalks not cleared from existing power poles at each end of property along right-of-way | Reviewer Convention | Only when new or modified sidewalks are proposed and existing power poles are located within or immediately adjacent to sidewalk alignment at property ends along ROW frontage. Check: Site plan for sidewalk alignment intersecting existing pole locations at property limits. |\n   104→| EL-1.26 | Enclosed trash areas and trash receptacles placed under overhead electric facilities creating risk of contact when trash bins are raised during service | Reviewer Convention | Only when trash enclosures or receptacle locations are shown beneath overhead electric facilities. Check: Site plan for trash enclosure locations beneath overhead lines or conductors; verify minimum 7.5-foot sky-to-ground clearance considering trash bin raised height during service. |\n   105→| EL-1.27 | Customer facilities proposed to be installed under or over Austin Energy overhead distribution facilities in violation of prohibition, or items within electric easements do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot clearance from outside conductor | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when customer facilities, building structures, site features, or overhangs are shown beneath, over, or within existing electric easement boundaries and overhead distribution facilities exist. Check: Easement exhibit and site plan for structures or features beneath/over overhead distribution lines (absolute prohibition regardless of clearance) or within electric easement (verify 7.5-foot clearance from outer conductor to all items); distinction from transmission facilities which have separate requirements under UCM § 1.14.0. |\n   106→| EL-1.28 | Water meters do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from outside conductor of existing overhead electric service drops | UCM § 1.10.3, UCM § 1.10.6.2 | Only when water meter locations are shown and overhead electric service lines (service-drop conductors from Austin Energy infrastructure to customer service point) exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Utility plan for water meter locations relative to overhead electric service drop routing and clearance dimensions. |\n   107→| EL-1.29 | Water lines do not maintain minimum clearance from existing overhead electrical distribution facilities | Reviewer Convention | Only when new water lines are shown and overhead electrical distribution facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Utility plan for water line routing beneath or adjacent to overhead distribution poles and conductors; verify clearance dimensions. |\n   108→| EL-1.30 | Permanent foundations within 5 feet of property line do not allow necessary clearance space for electric facilities | Reviewer Convention | Only when permanent structure foundations are shown within 5 feet of property line and electric facilities exist or are planned in adjacent ROW or on adjacent property. Check: Site plan for foundation locations within 5 feet of property line and utility plan for electric facility locations requiring clearance space. |\n   109→| EL-1.31 | Survey by registered surveyor showing pole locations and mid-span clearances not provided when existing overhead facilities may not meet minimum vertical clearance requirements | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when vertical clearance from overhead facilities to proposed site features (driveways, parking, buildings) appears marginal or insufficient based on site plan data. Check: Compare proposed site feature elevations to estimated overhead line heights—if clearance appears less than required minimums (7.5-foot sky-to-ground, 16-18 feet for driveways), verify registered surveyor mid-span clearance data provided. |\n   110→| EL-1.32 | Overhead electric line crossings of roadways not oriented at 90-degree angle to road | Reviewer Convention | Only when new overhead electric lines cross proposed or existing roadways. Check: Utility plan for overhead line crossing angles relative to roadway centerline alignments. |\n   111→| EL-1.33 | Secondary overhead electric lines cross over primary overhead electric lines | Reviewer Convention | Only when both secondary and primary overhead electric lines are shown on utility plans. Check: Utility plan section views or crossing details for line routing showing secondary above primary. |\n   112→| EL-1.34 | Marquee signs lack clearance verification or do not provide profile exhibits showing dimensions from sign to existing electrical poles including all conductors and neutral line | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when marquee signs are proposed and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Building elevation or site sections for marquee sign profile with clearance dimension annotations from electrical conductors (all types: primary, secondary, neutral); verify 7.5-foot sky-to-ground and 15-foot radial clearances. |\n   113→| EL-1.35 | New power poles for underground service located less than 20 feet from existing power poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when new power poles are proposed for underground service conversion and existing power poles will remain. Check: Utility plan for horizontal dimension between proposed new pole locations and existing poles to remain. |\n   114→| EL-1.36 | Decorative lighting proposed under existing overhead electric lines does not meet clearance requirements or is not relocated | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when decorative lighting fixtures are proposed and overhead electric lines exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Photometric plan or landscape plan for decorative light fixture locations beneath overhead lines and clearance verification dimensions (7.5-foot sky-to-ground, 15-foot radial) or relocation notes. |\n   115→| EL-1.37 | Trees proposed within 10 lateral feet of overhead distribution conductors or 50 lateral feet of overhead transmission conductors are not from Utility Compatible Shade Trees list, or site grading does not meet required clearances from transmission facilities | ECM § 2.4.1.D, ECM § 2.4.2.C, UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when landscape plans show tree plantings or grading modifications adjacent to or beneath electric facilities. Check: Landscape plan tree species against ECM Appendix F \"UC\" column (utility-compatible trees generally mature height ≤20 feet marked \"UC\"); measure lateral distance (horizontal) from tree locations to overhead conductors—within 10 lateral feet of distribution (voltage <60kV) requires utility-compatible species; within 50 lateral feet of transmission (voltage ≥60kV) requires utility-compatible species per UCM § 1.10.10.4 (note: ECM § 2.4.1.D and § 2.4.2.C reference 30-40 lateral feet, but UCM 50-foot standard is more restrictive and controls); grading contours and spot elevations near transmission structures must show clearance compliance annotations. |\n   116→| EL-1.38 | Proposed meter locations obstructed by site features, structures, or landscaping | UCM § 1.9.3.1 | Only when electric meter locations are shown on plans. Check: Site plan and enlarged meter area details for obstructions (fences, walls, equipment, vegetation, building projections) conflicting with meter access, installation, or required working clearances (30-inch-wide front space, 36-inch front depth, 6.5-foot headroom per UCM § 1.10.9). |\n   117→| EL-1.39 | Proposed development does not maintain required clearances from existing streetlights | Reviewer Convention | Only when new buildings or structures are proposed adjacent to existing streetlight poles. Check: Site plan for building and structure clearances from existing streetlight pole locations. |\n   118→| EL-1.40 | Down guy wires conflicting with accessible paths, driveways, or other site features not resolved through pole relocation or conversion to self-supporting steel poles | UCM § 1.17.0 | Only when down guy wires from existing electric poles (support cables from distribution poles to ground anchors, distinguished from angled guy wires on transmission towers) conflict with proposed accessible paths, driveways, or site features. Check: Site plan for guy wire locations (guy anchor symbols or notations near poles) intersecting proposed accessible routes or driveway alignments; verify relocation notes or conversion to self-supporting pole; note that electric utility infrastructure is frequently omitted from plans. |\n   119→| EL-1.41 | Guy easements for down guy anchors not provided extending 10 feet either side of guy wire plus 50 feet beyond end of anchor | Reviewer Convention | Only when angled guy anchors from transmission structures exist or will exist on site and require dedicated easements. Check: Easement exhibit for guy easement boundary showing 10 feet either side of guy wire alignment plus 50-foot extension beyond anchor point; distinguish from down guy easements (10-foot-wide extending 5 feet past ground entry). |\n   120→| EL-1.42 | Down guy anchor points lack dedicated easement 10 feet wide extending 5 feet past where down guy enters ground | Reviewer Convention | Only when down guy anchors from distribution poles exist or will exist on site. Check: Easement exhibit for down guy anchor easement showing 10-foot width and minimum 5-foot extension past anchor ground entry point; per Austin Energy Transmission Engineering Standards. |\n   121→| EL-1.43 | Transformers do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot vertical sky-to-ground clearance from overhead electric lines | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when transformers (pole-mounted or pad-mounted) are shown and overhead electric lines exist above or adjacent to transformer locations. Check: Utility plan section views for vertical clearance dimension from overhead line conductors to top of transformer equipment. |\n   122→| EL-1.44 | Transformers and switchgears in niche service configurations do not maintain minimum 35-foot vertical clearance inside niche and niche entrance | UCM § 1.10.4 | Only when transformers or switchgears are shown in niche service configurations (covered or recessed installations) with structures, canopies, or covered areas above equipment. Check: Section views showing vertical clearance dimension from top of equipment to bottom of overhead structure inside niche; note this 35-foot requirement applies to niche configurations only, not to all transformers/switchgears. |\n   123→| EL-1.45 | Construction staging areas, equipment operation zones, and work platforms do not maintain minimum 10-foot radial clearance from energized overhead electric lines per OSHA and Texas law | UCM § 1.10.2 | Only when construction staging areas, equipment laydown zones, or work platforms are shown on site logistics plans and overhead electric lines exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site logistics plan or construction phasing plan for staging area boundaries, crane swing radius, material storage, or scaffold locations; verify 10-foot radial clearance (all directions) from overhead lines maintained during construction; separate requirement from permanent clearances. |\n   124→| EL-1.46 | Plans do not include required note that owner/contractor must notify Austin Energy at least 48 hours before work begins near high-voltage overhead lines | UCM § 1.10.2 | Only when overhead electric lines exist on or adjacent to site and construction activities are proposed. Check: Cover sheet or general notes for 48-hour notification requirement per Texas Health & Safety Code Chapter 752, Section 752.003; note must specify notification to Austin Energy before work near high-voltage lines. |\n   125→| EL-1.47 | Service-mast through-roof installations do not maintain minimum 3-foot clearance above roof, or 18-inch clearance if within 4 feet of roof edge, for voltages ≤300V | UCM § 1.10.6.2 | Only when service-mast through-roof installations are shown on building elevations. Check: Building elevation for service mast location and roof clearance dimensions; verify 3-foot minimum above roof or 18-inch minimum if mast within 4 feet of roof edge; applies to voltages ≤300V only. |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7510649-3f32-4a6d-b604-08af9177b220","uuid":"3d8b40b9-ae84-4b05-b0f9-a13b7d79703c","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/1.md","content":"# Site Feature Clearances from Overhead Electric Lines\n\n## Description\n\nThis grouping covers clearance requirements between overhead electric distribution and transmission facilities (poles, conductors, guy wires) and proposed site features. Reviewers verify that buildings, fences, retaining walls, driveways, parking areas, sidewalks, lighting, utilities, and other site improvements maintain required horizontal and vertical clearances from existing or relocated overhead electric infrastructure.\n\n## Regulatory Overview\n\nAustin Energy's clearance requirements establish two simultaneous clearance envelopes that must both be satisfied: (1) a **7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance** that extends infinitely upward from ground level, creating a vertical wall at 7.5 feet from the conductor, and (2) a **15-foot radial clearance** measured in all directions from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors, creating a cylindrical zone around the conductor. Both requirements apply to all customer facilities—buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, and other installations. The code explicitly prohibits installing customer facilities under or over Austin Energy overhead distribution facilities, separate from maintaining clearance distances.\n\n**Distribution vs. Transmission Facilities:**\nThe distinction between distribution and transmission facilities is critical because they have different clearance and vegetation requirements. Distribution facilities operate at voltages below 60kV phase-to-phase (typically 12.47kV or 34.5kV in Austin), while transmission facilities operate at 60kV or higher (69kV, 138kV, or 345kV in Austin). Transmission facilities are physically larger—taller poles, wider structures, greater conductor spacing—and require more restrictive clearances. Development within 100 feet of transmission lines triggers specialized engineering analysis by Austin Energy transmission staff. Transmission facilities cannot be de-energized or easily relocated for private development, unlike distribution facilities.\n\n**Conductor Types:**\nUCM distinguishes between primary conductors (higher voltage distribution lines, typically 12.5kV), secondary conductors (lower voltage service lines), neutral conductors (grounded conductors in the distribution system), and service-drop conductors (overhead secondary voltage service from Austin Energy infrastructure to customer service point). The 15-foot radial clearance applies to overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors; the 7.5-foot sky-to-ground clearance applies to primary, neutral, and secondary conductors.\n\n**Key Thresholds:**\n- **7.5 feet horizontal sky-to-ground clearance** from overhead primary, neutral, and secondary conductors (UCM § 1.10.3)\n- **15-foot radius clearance** from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors, measured in all directions (UCM § 1.10.3)\n- **16 feet minimum vertical clearance** for driveways and parking without truck traffic (UCM § 1.10.6.2)\n- **18 feet minimum vertical clearance** for roads, streets, alleys, and areas with truck traffic (UCM § 1.10.6.2)\n- **10 lateral feet** from overhead distribution conductors for tree placement (ECM § 2.4.1.D, § 2.4.2.C)\n- **30-40 lateral feet** from overhead transmission conductors for tree placement (ECM § 2.4.1.D, § 2.4.2.C; UCM § 1.10.10.4 requires 50 lateral feet as more restrictive standard)\n- **10-foot radial clearance** for construction working clearances from energized overhead lines (OSHA 1910.333, UCM § 1.10.2)\n\n**Measurement Methodology (Critical):**\nAll clearances must be measured from the **outside conductor** (the actual electric wire), never from the utility pole centerline or pole structure. On multi-conductor installations, the outermost conductor may be several feet offset from pole center. Measuring from pole centerline instead of the conductor results in understated clearances and code violations. Profile plans must show dimension lines originating at the conductor location, not at the pole.\n\n**Exceptions:**\n- **Utility-compatible trees** (species marked \"UC\" in ECM Appendix F, generally mature height ≤20 feet) may be planted within 15 lateral feet of overhead distribution conductors where other trees cannot\n- **Service-drop roof clearances**: 18-inch minimum clearance above roof if service mast is within 4 feet of roof edge (instead of standard 3-foot clearance) for voltages ≤300V\n- **Transmission facility variations**: Austin Energy engineering staff may determine site-specific clearances for development within 100 feet of transmission lines using specialized analysis software and LiDAR survey data\n\n**Documentation Requirements:**\n- **Proximity surveys**: As required by Austin Energy Design, customer must provide survey showing proximities of existing and proposed facilities to existing Austin Energy primary voltage facilities (UCM § 1.10.3)\n- **Profile plans**: Engineered, sealed profile drawings showing vertical relationships between overhead conductors and building features, including overhangs, balconies, and canopies, with dimensioned clearances\n- **48-hour notification plan note**: Plans must include note that owner/contractor must notify Austin Energy at least 48 hours before work begins near high-voltage overhead lines (Texas Health & Safety Code Chapter 752, Section 752.003)\n\n**Common Violation Patterns:**\n- **Clearances measured from pole instead of conductor**: Dimension annotations read \"10'-0\" from pole\" instead of \"15'-0\" from outside conductor\"; conductor may be several feet offset from pole center on three-phase installations\n- **Missing profile plans**: Site plan shows building and overhead lines in plan view only, no elevation/section view; no surveyed mid-span clearance data; cannot verify vertical clearances meet requirements\n- **Facilities installed under/over overhead distribution**: Building footprint, canopy, sign, or structure shown directly beneath overhead conductor routing in violation of absolute prohibition regardless of clearance distances\n- **Non-utility-compatible trees beneath lines**: Landscape plan shows large trees beneath or within 10 feet of overhead lines; species not from Utility Compatible list in ECM Appendix F\n- **Inadequate service-drop clearances**: Driveway grade shown within 15-16 feet of overhead service drop without mid-span clearance verification; service drop sag at maximum load/temperature may contact vehicles\n- **Missing 48-hour notification note**: Site has overhead lines and construction staging area adjacent, but no plan notes addressing notification requirement\n- **Down guy conflicts**: Down guy anchors and guy wires omitted from plans; proposed accessible paths, driveways, or buildings shown on top of existing down guys\n\n## Code References\n\n- UCM § 1.10.0 - Clearance and Safety Requirements\n- UCM § 1.10.2 - Minimum Working Clearances From Energized Overhead Electric Utility Lines (OSHA)\n- UCM § 1.10.3 - Permanent Clearances From AE Overhead Distribution Lines and Facilities\n- UCM § 1.10.4 - Clearances From AE Padmount Equipment and Distribution Vaults\n- UCM § 1.10.6.2 - Service-Drop Conductor Clearances\n- UCM § 1.10.9 - Clearances for Metering Equipment and Enclosures\n- UCM § 1.9.2 - Clearances and Mounting Heights for Metering Equipment\n- UCM § 1.9.3.1 - Location of Meter Sockets\n- UCM § 1.14.0 - Transmission and Substation Requirements\n- UCM § 1.17.0 - Glossary (definitions of Distribution, Transmission, Infrastructure, Conductors)\n- ECM § 2.4.1.D - Street Yard Trees (tree placement near overhead conductors)\n- ECM § 2.4.2.C - Trees in Parking Lots (tree placement near overhead conductors)\n- ECM Appendix F - Descriptive Categories of Tree Species (Utility Compatible trees list)\n- NESC Section 234 - Horizontal sky-to-ground and radial clearances from overhead electric conductors (incorporated by UCM § 1.10.3)\n\n## Documents to Review\n\n- **Site Plan Sheets** - Existing overhead electric facilities (poles, conductors, guy wires, easements) and all proposed site features with clearance dimensions labeled\n- **Building Elevation Sheets** - Surveyed elevations of overhead conductors with clearance dimensions to building faces and projections\n- **Section/Profile Exhibits** - Cross-sections showing clearance dimensions from outer conductor to site features (buildings, walls, fences, driveways)\n- **Survey Data** - Registered surveyor data for pole locations, conductor elevations, mid-span clearances when vertical clearance verification needed\n- **Landscape Plans** - Site grading near overhead transmission facilities and proposed landscaping clearances; tree species verification against ECM Appendix F Utility Compatible list\n- **Utility Plans** - Existing electric pole locations, guy wires, conductor routing, easement boundaries with clearance annotations\n- **Cover Sheet** - Notes regarding property owner responsibility for maintaining clearances per NESC, OSHA, and UCM; 48-hour notification requirement to Austin Energy before work near high-voltage lines\n\n## Checklist Items\n\n| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n| EL-1.1 | Buildings and permanent structures do not maintain minimum 7 feet 6 inches horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from overhead electric conductors | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when buildings or permanent structures are proposed and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan for overhead poles/conductors and building locations; verify clearance measured from outside conductor, not pole. |\n| EL-1.2 | Customer facilities do not maintain minimum 15-foot radial clearance from outside conductor of overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when customer facilities/installations of any type (buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, or other installations) are proposed and overhead distribution facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan and utility plan for overhead distribution lines (voltage <60kV, typically 12.47kV or 34.5kV in Austin) and facility setbacks; verify conductor type labeled as primary or neutral. |\n| EL-1.3 | Building projections (overhangs, awnings, balconies, decks, roofs, patios) do not maintain required clearances from overhead electric conductors | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when building projections are shown and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Building elevations and section views for projections extending toward overhead conductors; verify both 7.5-foot sky-to-ground and 15-foot radial clearances from outside conductor. |\n| EL-1.4 | Gas pump canopies do not maintain required clearances from overhead electric lines | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when gas pump canopies are proposed and overhead electric lines exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan for gas pump canopy locations and overhead line routing; verify both 7.5-foot sky-to-ground and 15-foot radial clearances. |\n| EL-1.5 | Stairs, elevated walkways, and elevated platforms do not maintain minimum 15-foot radial clearance from overhead electric distribution facilities | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when stairs, elevated walkways, or elevated platforms are proposed and overhead electric distribution facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan and section views for elevated features near overhead lines; verify 15-foot radial clearance from outside conductor. |\n| EL-1.6 | Buildings do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance and 15-foot radial clearance from overhead electric facilities in right-of-way adjacent to site | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when buildings are proposed and electric facilities exist or will be relocated in ROW adjacent to site. Check: Site plan for building setback from ROW and utility plan for electric facility locations or relocation notes; verify clearances are permanent requirements per UCM § 1.10.3, distinct from construction working clearances. |\n| EL-1.7 | Surveyed elevations and locations of overhead conductors not included on building elevation sheets to demonstrate required clearances are met | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when buildings or elevated structures are proposed within potential clearance distance of overhead electric facilities. Check: Building elevation sheets for surveyed conductor elevation data and clearance dimension annotations; verify survey performed by registered surveyor. |\n| EL-1.8 | Section and plan view exhibits do not show dimensions from outer electric conductor to outermost edge of buildings and building projections for clearance verification | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when buildings, permanent structures, or building projections are proposed within potential clearance distance of overhead electric facilities. Check: Section views and enlarged plan details for dimension lines showing clearances from outer conductor to building edges. |\n| EL-1.9 | Fences do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from outside conductor of overhead electric facilities | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when fences are proposed and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan for fence alignments and dimension annotations showing clearance from outside conductor (not pole). |\n| EL-1.10 | Fence posts do not maintain minimum 10-foot horizontal clearance from overhead electric poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when fences are proposed and overhead electric poles exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan for fence post locations relative to electric pole locations. |\n| EL-1.11 | Fences connecting to or near substation fences not electrically isolated with 10-foot non-conductive isolation panel connecting the two fences | UCM § 1.14.0 | Only when fences are proposed adjacent to or connecting with existing Austin Energy substation fences. Check: Site plan for fence alignments near substation property and detail sheets for 10-foot non-conductive (wooden) panel specifications between metal fence and substation fence. |\n| EL-1.12 | Fencing near electric transmission lines not constructed of non-conductive material or not properly grounded, or specifications and grounding details not provided | UCM § 1.14.0 | Only when fencing is proposed beneath or adjacent to electric transmission lines (voltage ≥60kV). Check: Site plan for transmission line proximity to fences (facilities labeled 69kV, 138kV, or 345kV, or within transmission easement) and detail sheets for fence material specifications and grounding detail drawings. |\n| EL-1.13 | Vehicle barrier fencing beneath overhead electric lines does not maintain minimum 7.5-foot sky-to-ground clearance from outermost electric line or lacks vertical and horizontal clearance verification exhibits | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when vehicle barrier posts or fencing are proposed beneath or adjacent to overhead electric lines. Check: Site plan for barrier locations beneath overhead lines and section exhibits showing clearance dimensions from outermost conductor. |\n| EL-1.14 | Retaining walls do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from outermost electric line where overhead facilities are present | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when retaining walls are proposed and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan and grading plan for retaining wall alignments and clearance dimensions from outermost electric line (measured from conductor, not pole). |\n| EL-1.15 | Retaining walls adjacent to Austin Energy substation walls do not maintain minimum 5-foot separation to allow for grounding grid extension, wall inspection, wall replacement, and proper drainage | Reviewer Convention | Only when retaining walls are proposed adjacent to Austin Energy substation property. Check: Site plan for retaining wall locations within 10 feet of substation boundary and clearance dimension from substation wall. |\n| EL-1.16 | Emergency access structures (bridges, elevated platforms) do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot sky-to-ground clearance and 15-foot radial clearance from outside conductor of overhead electric facilities | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when emergency access structures such as bridges or elevated platforms are proposed and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Section views showing emergency access structure elevations relative to overhead conductor elevations; verify both clearance envelopes satisfied. |\n| EL-1.17 | Retaining walls lack section exhibits showing dimensions from outer electric conductor to outermost edge of wall for clearance verification | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when retaining walls are proposed within potential clearance distance of overhead electric facilities. Check: Section views for retaining wall clearance dimension annotations from outer conductor to wall face. |\n| EL-1.18 | Driveways do not maintain minimum 16-foot vertical clearance (parking without truck traffic) or 18-foot vertical clearance (areas with truck traffic) from lowest point of overhead electric facilities including telecommunications attachments | UCM § 1.10.6.2 | Only when driveways are proposed or modified and overhead electric facilities cross over or are adjacent to driveway locations. Check: Grading plan and section views showing finished driveway grade elevations and overhead line clearance dimensions; verify survey of mid-span clearances if needed; determine whether truck traffic expected to establish 16-foot vs. 18-foot requirement. |\n| EL-1.19 | Vehicles parked or displayed on concrete pads do not maintain minimum vertical clearance from lowest overhead electric lines, or requirement not noted on site plans | UCM § 1.10.6.2 | Only when vehicle parking or display areas on concrete pads are proposed beneath overhead electric lines. Check: Site plan and section views for parking pad locations beneath overhead lines, vertical clearance dimensions (12 feet for residential pedestrian areas, 16 feet for parking without trucks, 18 feet with truck traffic), and note about clearance requirement. |\n| EL-1.20 | Final road and driveway grades do not maintain minimum 30-foot vertical clearance from electric transmission lines to ground surface | UCM § 1.14.0 | Only when roads or driveways are proposed or regraded beneath electric transmission lines (voltage ≥60kV; facilities labeled 69kV, 138kV, or 345kV). Check: Grading plan and utility plan for transmission line locations (check voltage labels, transmission easement designations, large-scale structures) and final grade elevations with vertical clearance dimensions. |\n| EL-1.21 | Final highway and freeway grades with distribution underbuilt do not maintain minimum 40-foot vertical clearance from overhead electric lines to road surface | UCM § 1.14.0 | Only when highway or freeway grading is shown with existing distribution lines underbuilt on transmission structures. Check: Grading plan and cross-sections for roadway grades beneath transmission structures showing distribution underbuilt and 40-foot vertical clearance. |\n| EL-1.22 | Existing electric poles in conflict with proposed driveways not relocated minimum 30 feet from edge of driveway | Reviewer Convention | Only when proposed driveways are shown in conflict with or immediately adjacent to existing electric pole locations. Check: Site plan for driveway alignments within 30 feet of existing poles; verify relocation notes or revised pole locations. |\n| EL-1.23 | Clearance dimensions measured from utility pole or centerline instead of from outside conductor or outermost electric line as required by code | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when clearance dimensions are shown on plans and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Plan dimension annotations showing clearances—verify dimension lines extend from outside conductor (not pole center or centerline of three-phase lines); on multi-conductor installations, conductor may be several feet offset from pole center. |\n| EL-1.24 | Streetlight poles located under overhead electric lines | Reviewer Convention | Only when new streetlight poles are proposed and overhead electric lines exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan and photometric plan for streetlight pole locations beneath overhead line routing. |\n| EL-1.25 | Sidewalks not cleared from existing power poles at each end of property along right-of-way | Reviewer Convention | Only when new or modified sidewalks are proposed and existing power poles are located within or immediately adjacent to sidewalk alignment at property ends along ROW frontage. Check: Site plan for sidewalk alignment intersecting existing pole locations at property limits. |\n| EL-1.26 | Enclosed trash areas and trash receptacles placed under overhead electric facilities creating risk of contact when trash bins are raised during service | Reviewer Convention | Only when trash enclosures or receptacle locations are shown beneath overhead electric facilities. Check: Site plan for trash enclosure locations beneath overhead lines or conductors; verify minimum 7.5-foot sky-to-ground clearance considering trash bin raised height during service. |\n| EL-1.27 | Customer facilities proposed to be installed under or over Austin Energy overhead distribution facilities in violation of prohibition, or items within electric easements do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot clearance from outside conductor | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when customer facilities, building structures, site features, or overhangs are shown beneath, over, or within existing electric easement boundaries and overhead distribution facilities exist. Check: Easement exhibit and site plan for structures or features beneath/over overhead distribution lines (absolute prohibition regardless of clearance) or within electric easement (verify 7.5-foot clearance from outer conductor to all items); distinction from transmission facilities which have separate requirements under UCM § 1.14.0. |\n| EL-1.28 | Water meters do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from outside conductor of existing overhead electric service drops | UCM § 1.10.3, UCM § 1.10.6.2 | Only when water meter locations are shown and overhead electric service lines (service-drop conductors from Austin Energy infrastructure to customer service point) exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Utility plan for water meter locations relative to overhead electric service drop routing and clearance dimensions. |\n| EL-1.29 | Water lines do not maintain minimum clearance from existing overhead electrical distribution facilities | Reviewer Convention | Only when new water lines are shown and overhead electrical distribution facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Utility plan for water line routing beneath or adjacent to overhead distribution poles and conductors; verify clearance dimensions. |\n| EL-1.30 | Permanent foundations within 5 feet of property line do not allow necessary clearance space for electric facilities | Reviewer Convention | Only when permanent structure foundations are shown within 5 feet of property line and electric facilities exist or are planned in adjacent ROW or on adjacent property. Check: Site plan for foundation locations within 5 feet of property line and utility plan for electric facility locations requiring clearance space. |\n| EL-1.31 | Survey by registered surveyor showing pole locations and mid-span clearances not provided when existing overhead facilities may not meet minimum vertical clearance requirements | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when vertical clearance from overhead facilities to proposed site features (driveways, parking, buildings) appears marginal or insufficient based on site plan data. Check: Compare proposed site feature elevations to estimated overhead line heights—if clearance appears less than required minimums (7.5-foot sky-to-ground, 16-18 feet for driveways), verify registered surveyor mid-span clearance data provided. |\n| EL-1.32 | Overhead electric line crossings of roadways not oriented at 90-degree angle to road | Reviewer Convention | Only when new overhead electric lines cross proposed or existing roadways. Check: Utility plan for overhead line crossing angles relative to roadway centerline alignments. |\n| EL-1.33 | Secondary overhead electric lines cross over primary overhead electric lines | Reviewer Convention | Only when both secondary and primary overhead electric lines are shown on utility plans. Check: Utility plan section views or crossing details for line routing showing secondary above primary. |\n| EL-1.34 | Marquee signs lack clearance verification or do not provide profile exhibits showing dimensions from sign to existing electrical poles including all conductors and neutral line | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when marquee signs are proposed and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Building elevation or site sections for marquee sign profile with clearance dimension annotations from electrical conductors (all types: primary, secondary, neutral); verify 7.5-foot sky-to-ground and 15-foot radial clearances. |\n| EL-1.35 | New power poles for underground service located less than 20 feet from existing power poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when new power poles are proposed for underground service conversion and existing power poles will remain. Check: Utility plan for horizontal dimension between proposed new pole locations and existing poles to remain. |\n| EL-1.36 | Decorative lighting proposed under existing overhead electric lines does not meet clearance requirements or is not relocated | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when decorative lighting fixtures are proposed and overhead electric lines exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Photometric plan or landscape plan for decorative light fixture locations beneath overhead lines and clearance verification dimensions (7.5-foot sky-to-ground, 15-foot radial) or relocation notes. |\n| EL-1.37 | Trees proposed within 10 lateral feet of overhead distribution conductors or 50 lateral feet of overhead transmission conductors are not from Utility Compatible Shade Trees list, or site grading does not meet required clearances from transmission facilities | ECM § 2.4.1.D, ECM § 2.4.2.C, UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when landscape plans show tree plantings or grading modifications adjacent to or beneath electric facilities. Check: Landscape plan tree species against ECM Appendix F \"UC\" column (utility-compatible trees generally mature height ≤20 feet marked \"UC\"); measure lateral distance (horizontal) from tree locations to overhead conductors—within 10 lateral feet of distribution (voltage <60kV) requires utility-compatible species; within 50 lateral feet of transmission (voltage ≥60kV) requires utility-compatible species per UCM § 1.10.10.4 (note: ECM § 2.4.1.D and § 2.4.2.C reference 30-40 lateral feet, but UCM 50-foot standard is more restrictive and controls); grading contours and spot elevations near transmission structures must show clearance compliance annotations. |\n| EL-1.38 | Proposed meter locations obstructed by site features, structures, or landscaping | UCM § 1.9.3.1 | Only when electric meter locations are shown on plans. Check: Site plan and enlarged meter area details for obstructions (fences, walls, equipment, vegetation, building projections) conflicting with meter access, installation, or required working clearances (30-inch-wide front space, 36-inch front depth, 6.5-foot headroom per UCM § 1.10.9). |\n| EL-1.39 | Proposed development does not maintain required clearances from existing streetlights | Reviewer Convention | Only when new buildings or structures are proposed adjacent to existing streetlight poles. Check: Site plan for building and structure clearances from existing streetlight pole locations. |\n| EL-1.40 | Down guy wires conflicting with accessible paths, driveways, or other site features not resolved through pole relocation or conversion to self-supporting steel poles | UCM § 1.17.0 | Only when down guy wires from existing electric poles (support cables from distribution poles to ground anchors, distinguished from angled guy wires on transmission towers) conflict with proposed accessible paths, driveways, or site features. Check: Site plan for guy wire locations (guy anchor symbols or notations near poles) intersecting proposed accessible routes or driveway alignments; verify relocation notes or conversion to self-supporting pole; note that electric utility infrastructure is frequently omitted from plans. |\n| EL-1.41 | Guy easements for down guy anchors not provided extending 10 feet either side of guy wire plus 50 feet beyond end of anchor | Reviewer Convention | Only when angled guy anchors from transmission structures exist or will exist on site and require dedicated easements. Check: Easement exhibit for guy easement boundary showing 10 feet either side of guy wire alignment plus 50-foot extension beyond anchor point; distinguish from down guy easements (10-foot-wide extending 5 feet past ground entry). |\n| EL-1.42 | Down guy anchor points lack dedicated easement 10 feet wide extending 5 feet past where down guy enters ground | Reviewer Convention | Only when down guy anchors from distribution poles exist or will exist on site. Check: Easement exhibit for down guy anchor easement showing 10-foot width and minimum 5-foot extension past anchor ground entry point; per Austin Energy Transmission Engineering Standards. |\n| EL-1.43 | Transformers do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot vertical sky-to-ground clearance from overhead electric lines | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when transformers (pole-mounted or pad-mounted) are shown and overhead electric lines exist above or adjacent to transformer locations. Check: Utility plan section views for vertical clearance dimension from overhead line conductors to top of transformer equipment. |\n| EL-1.44 | Transformers and switchgears in niche service configurations do not maintain minimum 35-foot vertical clearance inside niche and niche entrance | UCM § 1.10.4 | Only when transformers or switchgears are shown in niche service configurations (covered or recessed installations) with structures, canopies, or covered areas above equipment. Check: Section views showing vertical clearance dimension from top of equipment to bottom of overhead structure inside niche; note this 35-foot requirement applies to niche configurations only, not to all transformers/switchgears. |\n| EL-1.45 | Construction staging areas, equipment operation zones, and work platforms do not maintain minimum 10-foot radial clearance from energized overhead electric lines per OSHA and Texas law | UCM § 1.10.2 | Only when construction staging areas, equipment laydown zones, or work platforms are shown on site logistics plans and overhead electric lines exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site logistics plan or construction phasing plan for staging area boundaries, crane swing radius, material storage, or scaffold locations; verify 10-foot radial clearance (all directions) from overhead lines maintained during construction; separate requirement from permanent clearances. |\n| EL-1.46 | Plans do not include required note that owner/contractor must notify Austin Energy at least 48 hours before work begins near high-voltage overhead lines | UCM § 1.10.2 | Only when overhead electric lines exist on or adjacent to site and construction activities are proposed. Check: Cover sheet or general notes for 48-hour notification requirement per Texas Health & Safety Code Chapter 752, Section 752.003; note must specify notification to Austin Energy before work near high-voltage lines. |\n| EL-1.47 | Service-mast through-roof installations do not maintain minimum 3-foot clearance above roof, or 18-inch clearance if within 4 feet of roof edge, for voltages ≤300V | UCM § 1.10.6.2 | Only when service-mast through-roof installations are shown on building elevations. Check: Building elevation for service mast location and roof clearance dimensions; verify 3-foot minimum above roof or 18-inch minimum if mast within 4 feet of roof edge; applies to voltages ≤300V only. |","numLines":125,"startLine":1,"totalLines":125}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778188306524,"pid":122,"hostname":"e70bc6df-98e","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":8,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR24GEYVQRSQE15N7HNMN942","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YSr15qMwUyEdqwrWF6vw1j","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/13.md"}}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"21ef172e-3b37-47d1-a102-ca3d2cea074d","uuid":"e12d1117-79f2-49da-a58e-f11ac179fbee"}
{"level":30,"time":1778188306574,"pid":122,"hostname":"e70bc6df-98e","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":8,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YSr15qMwUyEdqwrWF6vw1j","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Transformer Pad Clearances and Location Requirements\n     2→\n     3→## Description\n     4→\n     5→This grouping covers spatial and location requirements for transformer pad installations, including horizontal clearances from buildings and site features, vertical clearances for equipment access and overhead obstructions, separation from underground utilities, property location restrictions and prohibited areas, and transformer pad sizing and protection standards.\n     6→\n     7→## Regulatory Overview\n     8→\n     9→Transformer pad clearances are governed by a multi-layered framework in the Utilities Criteria Manual (UCM) that addresses horizontal clearances from structures and site features, vertical clearances for crane access and overhead obstructions, separation from underground utilities, and property/easement location restrictions. The primary regulatory structure is UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4, which establishes clearance requirements based on equipment orientation, building materials, and spatial configuration.\n    10→\n    11→**Pad-Mounted Transformers:** Electrical transformers installed at ground level on concrete foundation pads (as distinguished from pole-mounted or vault transformers). They contain pad-locked access doors and are supplied in both single-phase and three-phase configurations. Specific pad dimensions are determined by Austin Energy Design on a project-specific basis, not standardized in code.\n    12→\n    13→**Three-Phase vs. Single-Phase Service:** Service type is determined by Austin Energy Design based on customer electrical load requirements and infrastructure availability, not from civil site plans alone. Three-phase service is furnished only where the customer's load and equipment warrant it and where AE has necessary facilities installed and available (UCM § 1.3.9).\n    14→\n    15→**Key Thresholds:**\n    16→\n    17→- **Hot-stick use area (access door side):** 10 feet to open area or adjacent building/structure; **15 feet when facing confined space** (niche, vault, or enclosed area on 3+ sides)\n    18→- **Sides with operators or controls:** 5 feet from removable ventilated obstruction (fence)\n    19→- **Sides without operators or controls:** 3 feet from removable ventilated obstruction (fence)\n    20→- **Fire escape clearance:** 20 feet horizontal/radial from edge of oil-filled equipment pad to fire escape or stairs serving as fire escape\n    21→- **Brick or masonry buildings:** 5 feet minimum horizontal clearance from side edges of equipment pad\n    22→- **Windows/doors/ventilating ducts:** 5 feet minimum lateral clearance (brick/masonry); 12 feet horizontal from edge of oil-filled equipment pad if window/door is less than 12 feet from grade or has less than 5 feet lateral separation\n    23→- **Non-brick/masonry structures:** 12 feet horizontal clearance from edge of oil-filled equipment pad\n    24→- **Niche vertical clearance:** Minimum 35 feet vertical clearance inside niche and niche entrance (not 20 or 30 feet)\n    25→- **Overhead structures:** NO covering, building, or structure allowed directly above pad or minimum equipment area\n    26→- **Sidewalks:** 2 feet from back of sidewalks\n    27→- **Overhead distribution lines:** Minimum 7.5 feet horizontal sky-to-ground clearance; **15-foot radius clearance** from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors\n    28→- **Underground utilities:** 12 inches minimum (horizontal and vertical) from other utilities; 24-36 inches from gas lines; 5 feet from Austin Water infrastructure\n    29→- **Trees:** 10 lateral feet from any side of pad-mounted equipment; shrubs 10 lateral feet from access door side, 5 lateral feet from other sides\n    30→- **Root barriers:** Required for trees within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment (4 feet deep, 5 feet from equipment)\n    31→- **Bollards:** 4-inch minimum diameter galvanized rigid metal posts required when equipment pad installed **within 4 feet** of parking/traffic areas (not \"3 feet in front\")\n    32→\n    33→**Exceptions:**\n    34→\n    35→- **3-foot clearance reduction:** 5-foot clearance to brick/masonry buildings may be reduced to 3 feet (for one side only) with Austin Energy Design written approval when: (1) not in confined space, (2) 10 ft+ to windows/doors/ducts, (3) 15 ft+ vertical clearance to windows/doors/ducts, and (4) minimum 3-hour fire rating in clearance area (UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 2)\n    36→- **Niche service:** Specialized configuration where AE equipment is accessible from outside but located within customer's building footprint. Permitted only outside the downtown Network Area. Requires 35-foot vertical clearance, 3-hour fire walls, proper ventilation, and consultation with AE Design business unit (UCM § 1.10.4 footnote 3)\n    37→- **Residential service line separations:** Minimum 3-foot horizontal separation may be allowed between water, reclaimed water, and wastewater service lines and dry utility service lines on residential projects (UCM § 2.9.1.B.2)\n    38→\n    39→**Property and Easement Requirements:**\n    40→\n    41→- All padmount/vault/niche equipment must be installed on customer's property, not in public right-of-way (UCM § 1.10.4 footnote 4)\n    42→- Electrical routing from transformer to meter must be 100% on private property\n    43→- Transformer easements required around pads (width determined by AE Design for accessibility)\n    44→- Equipment cannot be placed in drainage easements, water easements, sewer easements, or electrical easements under overhead lines without AE Design written approval\n    45→\n    46→**Calculation Methods:**\n    47→\n    48→- **Horizontal clearances:** Measured from outside edges of equipment pad to adjacent features (buildings, structures, site amenities)\n    49→- **Lateral clearances:** Measured horizontally from specific building features (windows, doors, ventilating ducts)\n    50→- **Radial clearances:** Measured from center point or edge of equipment in all directions (fire escapes, overhead conductors)\n    51→- **Vertical clearances:** Measured from grade level upward (windows requiring 12 feet when lateral clearance < 5 feet), or from underside of overhead structures downward (35 feet for niches, crane access)\n    52→- **Underground utility separations:** Measured outside diameter to outside diameter (OD to OD) of pipes/conduits\n    53→\n    54→**Common Violation Patterns:**\n    55→\n    56→- **3-foot fence clearance shown without AE Design written approval:** Plans show 3-foot clearance to building without documenting: (1) written AE Design approval, (2) verification of 3-hour fire rating, (3) confirmation not in confined space, and (4) adequate vertical clearances to windows/doors\n    57→- **Niche/enclosed areas without 35-foot clearance documentation:** Plans show transformer in niche, enclosed area, or notched-out building configuration without cross-section drawings demonstrating 35-foot vertical clearance from grade through entire access path\n    58→- **Transformer pad dimensions not matching AE Design details:** Plans show pad dimensions (e.g., \"5'x5'\" or \"10'x10'\") that do not match AE Design-approved transformer pad detail sheets for the specified service type and load\n    59→- **Bollards missing when pad within 4 feet of parking:** Plans show transformer pad located within 4 feet of parking aisles, drive lanes, or vehicle circulation areas without 4-inch diameter bollards per AE Design equipment pad details\n    60→- **Drainage sloping toward building from oil-filled equipment:** Grading plan shows drainage slopes or surface flow directed toward building from transformer pad location (code requires liquid flow away from building for oil-filled equipment per UCM § 1.10.4 footnote 1)\n    61→- **15-foot radius violation for overhead conductors:** Plans show structures, landscaping, or site features within 15-foot radius of overhead distribution primary/neutral conductors (measured from conductors, not pole centerline)\n    62→- **Trees within 10 feet not identified as utility-compatible:** Landscape plan shows trees within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted equipment or within 15 lateral feet of overhead distribution conductors without species confirmation from ECM Appendix F \"UC\" (Utility Compatible) column\n    63→- **Fire rating not documented for reduced clearances:** Plans show clearances less than 5 feet to buildings without architectural specifications confirming minimum 3-hour fire rating in the clearance area\n    64→\n    65→**Confined Space / Niche Identification:**\n    66→\n    67→A transformer location qualifies as \"facing a confined space\" (triggering 15-foot clearance instead of 10-foot) when the access door side faces an area enclosed on 3 or more sides with limited ventilation or restricted egress. Niches are specialized installations where AE equipment is accessible from outside but located within the customer's building footprint, requiring 35-foot vertical clearance, 3-hour fire walls, and AE Design consultation.\n    68→\n    69→**Oil-Filled vs. Dry-Type Equipment:**\n    70→\n    71→Certain Table 1.10.4 clearances specify increased requirements for \"oil-filled equipment\" due to fire risk from insulating oil/liquid. Oil-filled transformer types should be identified on electrical specifications or transformer callouts. Fire escape clearances (20 feet) and grading/drainage direction requirements (liquid flow away from building) apply specifically to oil-filled equipment.\n    72→\n    73→## Code References\n    74→\n    75→- UCM § 1.10.4 - Clearances From AE Padmount Equipment and Distribution Vaults (primary governing section)\n    76→- UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 - Minimum Clearances from Padmount Equipment Pads and Distribution Vaults\n    77→- UCM § 1.10.5 - Clearances for AE Underground Cable, Conduit, and Underground Facilities\n    78→- UCM § 1.10.3 - Permanent Clearances from AE Overhead Distribution Lines and Facilities\n    79→- UCM § 1.10.10.4 - Trees in Proximity to Electric Utility Infrastructure\n    80→- UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 - Horizontal and Vertical Separation Distance (Austin Water infrastructure)\n    81→- UCM § 1.3.9 - Single-Phase/Three-Phase Service\n    82→- UCM § 1.5.2.6 - Pad-Mounted Transformer Service\n    83→- UCM § 1.18.0 - Appendix and Exhibits\n    84→- ECM Appendix F - Descriptive Categories of Tree Species (utility-compatible tree identification)\n    85→\n    86→## Documents to Review\n    87→\n    88→- **Site Plan Sheets** - Transformer pad locations, dimensions, clearances to buildings and site features, property lines\n    89→- **Electrical Plan Sheets** - Transformer locations, electrical routing to meters, transformer easements, service type specifications\n    90→- **Utility Plan Sheets** - Underground utility locations, conflicts with transformer pads, utility crossings, overhead line locations\n    91→- **Landscape Plan Sheets** - Tree species identification, vegetation proximity to transformer pads, root barrier details\n    92→- **Detail Sheets** - Transformer pad construction details matching AE Design-approved specifications, bollard details, protective barrier specifications\n    93→- **Easement Exhibits** - Transformer easements, drainage easements, utility easements, prohibited areas, property boundaries\n    94→- **Building Elevation Sheets** - Vertical clearances from windows, balconies, overhangs, fire escape locations\n    95→- **Cross-Section Drawings** - Vertical clearances from overhead structures, parking garages, enclosed areas, niche configurations showing 35-foot clearance\n    96→- **Grading and Drainage Plans** - Surface drainage direction around transformer pads (for oil-filled equipment)\n    97→- **Architectural Specifications** - Fire ratings for walls/structures in reduced clearance areas (3-hour rating verification)\n    98→- **Austin Energy Design Approval Documents** - ESPA (Electric Service Planning Application), transformer pad detail sheets, service type approval, written approvals for clearance reductions or variances\n    99→\n   100→## Checklist Items\n   101→\n   102→| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n   103→|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n   104→| EL-13.1 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from buildings, structures, and building foundations | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4; UCM § 1.10.5.B | All sites |\n   105→| EL-13.2 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from retaining walls | Reviewer Convention | Only when retaining walls are present. Check: site plan and grading plan. |\n   106→| EL-13.3 | Transformer pads lack required clearance from fences and gates - 5 feet for sides with operators/controls, 3 feet for sides without operators/controls (from removable ventilated fences) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when fences or gates are adjacent to transformer pads. Check: site plan showing fence locations and transformer access door orientation. |\n   107→| EL-13.4 | Transformer pads with reduced clearances (3 feet to brick/masonry buildings) lack documentation of Austin Energy Design written approval or do not meet required conditions (not in confined space, 10 ft+ to windows/doors/ducts, 15 ft+ vertical clearance to windows/doors/ducts, 3-hour fire rating) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 2 | Only when clearances less than 5 feet to buildings are shown. Check: site plan dimensions and architectural specifications for fire rating. |\n   108→| EL-13.5 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot lateral clearance from balconies and building overhangs, or have coverings/structures directly above pad or minimum equipment area | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when balconies or overhangs are present above or adjacent to transformer. Check: site plan, building elevations, and building cross-sections. |\n   109→| EL-13.6 | Transformer pads lack minimum 12-foot horizontal clearance from windows, doors, or ventilating ducts when window/door/duct is less than 12 feet from grade or has less than 5 feet lateral separation (oil-filled equipment) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when windows, doors, or ventilating ducts are present on adjacent buildings with inadequate lateral separation or low grade elevation. Check: site plan, building elevations, and transformer equipment specifications. |\n   110→| EL-13.7 | Transformer pads not located minimum 2 feet from back of sidewalks | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when sidewalks are adjacent to transformer pads. Check: site plan. |\n   111→| EL-13.8 | Transformer pads lack required clearance from stairwells serving as fire exits | Reviewer Convention | Only when stairwells are present adjacent to transformer. Check: site plan and building floor plans. |\n   112→| EL-13.9 | Transformer pads lack minimum 20-foot horizontal/radial clearance from fire escapes or stairs serving as fire escapes (oil-filled equipment) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when fire escapes or stairs serving as fire escapes are present. Check: site plan and building elevations showing fire escape locations. |\n   113→| EL-13.10 | Transformer pad hot-stick use area (access door side) lacks minimum 10-foot clearance to open area or adjacent building/structure | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | All sites |\n   114→| EL-13.11 | Transformer pad hot-stick use area (access door side) lacks minimum 15-foot clearance when facing confined space (niche, vault, or enclosed area on 3+ sides) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when transformer location faces confined space such as niche, vault, or notched-out building area. Check: site plan configuration and building cross-sections. |\n   115→| EL-13.12 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from fire hydrants | Reviewer Convention | Only when fire hydrants are present. Check: site plan showing fire hydrant locations. |\n   116→| EL-13.13 | Trees within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted equipment are not identified as utility-compatible species per ECM Appendix F, or trees within 15 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductors are not utility-compatible | UCM § 1.10.10.4; ECM Appendix F | Only when trees are proposed within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted equipment or 15 lateral feet of overhead distribution. Check: landscape plan species list cross-referenced with ECM Appendix F \"UC\" column. |\n   117→| EL-13.14 | Shrubs or low-lying vegetation lack minimum 10 lateral feet clearance from transformer access door side, or lack minimum 5 lateral feet clearance from other sides (non-access door) | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when shrubs, vegetation, or landscaping is adjacent to transformer. Check: landscape plan showing planting locations relative to transformer access door orientation. |\n   118→| EL-13.15 | Trees within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment lack required root barriers (minimum 4 feet deep, installed 5 feet from equipment) | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when trees are proposed within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment. Check: landscape plan and detail sheets for root barrier specifications. |\n   119→| EL-13.16 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from dumpsters | Reviewer Convention | Only when dumpsters are present. Check: site plan showing dumpster locations and enclosures. |\n   120→| EL-13.17 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from bike racks | Reviewer Convention | Only when bike racks are present. Check: site plan. |\n   121→| EL-13.18 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from light poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when light poles are adjacent to transformer. Check: site plan and photometric plan. |\n   122→| EL-13.19 | Transformer pads lack minimum 15-foot clearance from fire lanes | Reviewer Convention | Only when fire lanes are designated. Check: site plan showing fire lane striping, signage, and plan notes. |\n   123→| EL-13.20 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from other transformer pads | Reviewer Convention | Only when multiple transformer pads are proposed. Check: electrical plan. |\n   124→| EL-13.21 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot horizontal clearance from water lines | UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 | All sites |\n   125→| EL-13.22 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot horizontal clearance from wastewater lines | UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 | All sites |\n   126→| EL-13.23 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot horizontal clearance from storm drain lines | UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 | All sites |\n   127→| EL-13.24 | Transformer pads lack minimum horizontal clearance from gas lines (24 inches minimum for gas pipelines less than 60 psi, 36 inches minimum for high-pressure gas 60 psi and over) | UCM § 1.10.5.A | Only when gas service is present. Check: utility plan showing gas infrastructure and pressure specifications. |\n   128→| EL-13.25 | Transformer pads lack minimum 12-inch horizontal and vertical clearance from other underground utilities (except gas, fuel, steam) | UCM § 1.10.5.A | All sites |\n   129→| EL-13.26 | Transformer pads or AE underground facilities located over or under other utility lines, or customer buildings/foundations/structures installed over AE underground facilities | UCM § 1.10.5.B | All sites |\n   130→| EL-13.27 | Transformer locations lack minimum 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from overhead distribution line conductors (primary, neutral, and secondary), extending from ground to sky | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution lines are present. Check: site plan and utility plan showing overhead lines and proposed structures/features. |\n   131→| EL-13.28 | Transformer locations or proposed facilities lack minimum 15-foot radius clearance from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors (measured from conductors, not pole centerline) | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution primary or neutral conductors are present. Check: utility plan showing overhead line locations and proposed site features. |\n   132→| EL-13.29 | Transformer locations in enclosed or notched-out areas (niches) lack minimum 35-foot vertical clearance inside niche and niche entrance, or lack cross-section drawings demonstrating clearance | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 3 | Only when transformer is located in niche (accessible from outside but within building footprint), enclosed area, or notched-out building configuration. Check: site plan configuration, building cross-sections, and niche detail drawings. |\n   133→| EL-13.30 | Niche service installations lack required 3-hour fire wall rating for walls and ceilings, proper ventilation specifications, or documentation of consultation with Austin Energy Design business unit | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 3 | Only when niche service is proposed (equipment within building footprint, accessible from outside). Check: architectural specifications and electrical plan notes for AE Design coordination. |\n   134→| EL-13.31 | Cross-section drawings not provided showing vertical clearances in access paths with overhead structures, parking garages, or enclosed areas | UCM § 1.10.3 (survey requirement as required by AE Design) | Only when overhead structures, parking garages, enclosed areas, or niches are present above or adjacent to transformer locations. Check: plan set for cross-section sheets demonstrating vertical clearance compliance. |\n   135→| EL-13.32 | Grading plan shows drainage slopes toward building from oil-filled transformer pad location (liquid flow must be away from building) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 1 | Only when oil-filled transformer equipment is proposed. Check: grading and drainage plans showing surface flow direction around transformer pad, and transformer equipment specifications. |\n   136→| EL-13.33 | Transformer pads not located 100% on private property, located in public right-of-way, or straddling property lines | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 4 | All sites |\n   137→| EL-13.34 | Transformer pads located in prohibited easements including drainage easements, water easements, sewer easements, or electrical easements under overhead lines without Austin Energy Design written approval | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 4 (property requirement); easement-specific regulations | All sites |\n   138→| EL-13.35 | Transformer easements not shown around pads, or easement width inadequate for Austin Energy accessibility requirements | UCM § 1.10.3 (additional clearance and access easements may be required) | All sites |\n   139→| EL-13.36 | Electrical routing from transformer to meter not 100% on private property | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 4 | All sites |\n   140→| EL-13.37 | Transformer pad dimensions do not match Austin Energy Design-approved transformer pad detail sheets for specified service type (single-phase or three-phase) | UCM § 1.5.2.6; UCM § 1.18.0 (obtain transformer pad detail from AE Design) | All sites |\n   141→| EL-13.38 | Equipment pad installed within 4 feet of parking areas or vehicle routes lacks required 4-inch minimum diameter galvanized rigid metal posts (bollards) per AE Design equipment pad details, or bollards not spaced to allow equipment door opening | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when transformer pad is located within 4 feet of parking areas, drive aisles, or vehicle circulation routes. Check: site plan showing parking layout, circulation, and bollard locations/specifications. |\n   142→| EL-13.39 | Customer facilities (buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks) installed under or over Austin Energy overhead distribution facilities | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution facilities are present. Check: site plan and utility plan showing overhead line locations and proposed structures. |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"21ef172e-3b37-47d1-a102-ca3d2cea074d","uuid":"ba5c87d9-3668-4472-8bb0-3512595bc146","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/13.md","content":"# Transformer Pad Clearances and Location Requirements\n\n## Description\n\nThis grouping covers spatial and location requirements for transformer pad installations, including horizontal clearances from buildings and site features, vertical clearances for equipment access and overhead obstructions, separation from underground utilities, property location restrictions and prohibited areas, and transformer pad sizing and protection standards.\n\n## Regulatory Overview\n\nTransformer pad clearances are governed by a multi-layered framework in the Utilities Criteria Manual (UCM) that addresses horizontal clearances from structures and site features, vertical clearances for crane access and overhead obstructions, separation from underground utilities, and property/easement location restrictions. The primary regulatory structure is UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4, which establishes clearance requirements based on equipment orientation, building materials, and spatial configuration.\n\n**Pad-Mounted Transformers:** Electrical transformers installed at ground level on concrete foundation pads (as distinguished from pole-mounted or vault transformers). They contain pad-locked access doors and are supplied in both single-phase and three-phase configurations. Specific pad dimensions are determined by Austin Energy Design on a project-specific basis, not standardized in code.\n\n**Three-Phase vs. Single-Phase Service:** Service type is determined by Austin Energy Design based on customer electrical load requirements and infrastructure availability, not from civil site plans alone. Three-phase service is furnished only where the customer's load and equipment warrant it and where AE has necessary facilities installed and available (UCM § 1.3.9).\n\n**Key Thresholds:**\n\n- **Hot-stick use area (access door side):** 10 feet to open area or adjacent building/structure; **15 feet when facing confined space** (niche, vault, or enclosed area on 3+ sides)\n- **Sides with operators or controls:** 5 feet from removable ventilated obstruction (fence)\n- **Sides without operators or controls:** 3 feet from removable ventilated obstruction (fence)\n- **Fire escape clearance:** 20 feet horizontal/radial from edge of oil-filled equipment pad to fire escape or stairs serving as fire escape\n- **Brick or masonry buildings:** 5 feet minimum horizontal clearance from side edges of equipment pad\n- **Windows/doors/ventilating ducts:** 5 feet minimum lateral clearance (brick/masonry); 12 feet horizontal from edge of oil-filled equipment pad if window/door is less than 12 feet from grade or has less than 5 feet lateral separation\n- **Non-brick/masonry structures:** 12 feet horizontal clearance from edge of oil-filled equipment pad\n- **Niche vertical clearance:** Minimum 35 feet vertical clearance inside niche and niche entrance (not 20 or 30 feet)\n- **Overhead structures:** NO covering, building, or structure allowed directly above pad or minimum equipment area\n- **Sidewalks:** 2 feet from back of sidewalks\n- **Overhead distribution lines:** Minimum 7.5 feet horizontal sky-to-ground clearance; **15-foot radius clearance** from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors\n- **Underground utilities:** 12 inches minimum (horizontal and vertical) from other utilities; 24-36 inches from gas lines; 5 feet from Austin Water infrastructure\n- **Trees:** 10 lateral feet from any side of pad-mounted equipment; shrubs 10 lateral feet from access door side, 5 lateral feet from other sides\n- **Root barriers:** Required for trees within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment (4 feet deep, 5 feet from equipment)\n- **Bollards:** 4-inch minimum diameter galvanized rigid metal posts required when equipment pad installed **within 4 feet** of parking/traffic areas (not \"3 feet in front\")\n\n**Exceptions:**\n\n- **3-foot clearance reduction:** 5-foot clearance to brick/masonry buildings may be reduced to 3 feet (for one side only) with Austin Energy Design written approval when: (1) not in confined space, (2) 10 ft+ to windows/doors/ducts, (3) 15 ft+ vertical clearance to windows/doors/ducts, and (4) minimum 3-hour fire rating in clearance area (UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 2)\n- **Niche service:** Specialized configuration where AE equipment is accessible from outside but located within customer's building footprint. Permitted only outside the downtown Network Area. Requires 35-foot vertical clearance, 3-hour fire walls, proper ventilation, and consultation with AE Design business unit (UCM § 1.10.4 footnote 3)\n- **Residential service line separations:** Minimum 3-foot horizontal separation may be allowed between water, reclaimed water, and wastewater service lines and dry utility service lines on residential projects (UCM § 2.9.1.B.2)\n\n**Property and Easement Requirements:**\n\n- All padmount/vault/niche equipment must be installed on customer's property, not in public right-of-way (UCM § 1.10.4 footnote 4)\n- Electrical routing from transformer to meter must be 100% on private property\n- Transformer easements required around pads (width determined by AE Design for accessibility)\n- Equipment cannot be placed in drainage easements, water easements, sewer easements, or electrical easements under overhead lines without AE Design written approval\n\n**Calculation Methods:**\n\n- **Horizontal clearances:** Measured from outside edges of equipment pad to adjacent features (buildings, structures, site amenities)\n- **Lateral clearances:** Measured horizontally from specific building features (windows, doors, ventilating ducts)\n- **Radial clearances:** Measured from center point or edge of equipment in all directions (fire escapes, overhead conductors)\n- **Vertical clearances:** Measured from grade level upward (windows requiring 12 feet when lateral clearance < 5 feet), or from underside of overhead structures downward (35 feet for niches, crane access)\n- **Underground utility separations:** Measured outside diameter to outside diameter (OD to OD) of pipes/conduits\n\n**Common Violation Patterns:**\n\n- **3-foot fence clearance shown without AE Design written approval:** Plans show 3-foot clearance to building without documenting: (1) written AE Design approval, (2) verification of 3-hour fire rating, (3) confirmation not in confined space, and (4) adequate vertical clearances to windows/doors\n- **Niche/enclosed areas without 35-foot clearance documentation:** Plans show transformer in niche, enclosed area, or notched-out building configuration without cross-section drawings demonstrating 35-foot vertical clearance from grade through entire access path\n- **Transformer pad dimensions not matching AE Design details:** Plans show pad dimensions (e.g., \"5'x5'\" or \"10'x10'\") that do not match AE Design-approved transformer pad detail sheets for the specified service type and load\n- **Bollards missing when pad within 4 feet of parking:** Plans show transformer pad located within 4 feet of parking aisles, drive lanes, or vehicle circulation areas without 4-inch diameter bollards per AE Design equipment pad details\n- **Drainage sloping toward building from oil-filled equipment:** Grading plan shows drainage slopes or surface flow directed toward building from transformer pad location (code requires liquid flow away from building for oil-filled equipment per UCM § 1.10.4 footnote 1)\n- **15-foot radius violation for overhead conductors:** Plans show structures, landscaping, or site features within 15-foot radius of overhead distribution primary/neutral conductors (measured from conductors, not pole centerline)\n- **Trees within 10 feet not identified as utility-compatible:** Landscape plan shows trees within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted equipment or within 15 lateral feet of overhead distribution conductors without species confirmation from ECM Appendix F \"UC\" (Utility Compatible) column\n- **Fire rating not documented for reduced clearances:** Plans show clearances less than 5 feet to buildings without architectural specifications confirming minimum 3-hour fire rating in the clearance area\n\n**Confined Space / Niche Identification:**\n\nA transformer location qualifies as \"facing a confined space\" (triggering 15-foot clearance instead of 10-foot) when the access door side faces an area enclosed on 3 or more sides with limited ventilation or restricted egress. Niches are specialized installations where AE equipment is accessible from outside but located within the customer's building footprint, requiring 35-foot vertical clearance, 3-hour fire walls, and AE Design consultation.\n\n**Oil-Filled vs. Dry-Type Equipment:**\n\nCertain Table 1.10.4 clearances specify increased requirements for \"oil-filled equipment\" due to fire risk from insulating oil/liquid. Oil-filled transformer types should be identified on electrical specifications or transformer callouts. Fire escape clearances (20 feet) and grading/drainage direction requirements (liquid flow away from building) apply specifically to oil-filled equipment.\n\n## Code References\n\n- UCM § 1.10.4 - Clearances From AE Padmount Equipment and Distribution Vaults (primary governing section)\n- UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 - Minimum Clearances from Padmount Equipment Pads and Distribution Vaults\n- UCM § 1.10.5 - Clearances for AE Underground Cable, Conduit, and Underground Facilities\n- UCM § 1.10.3 - Permanent Clearances from AE Overhead Distribution Lines and Facilities\n- UCM § 1.10.10.4 - Trees in Proximity to Electric Utility Infrastructure\n- UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 - Horizontal and Vertical Separation Distance (Austin Water infrastructure)\n- UCM § 1.3.9 - Single-Phase/Three-Phase Service\n- UCM § 1.5.2.6 - Pad-Mounted Transformer Service\n- UCM § 1.18.0 - Appendix and Exhibits\n- ECM Appendix F - Descriptive Categories of Tree Species (utility-compatible tree identification)\n\n## Documents to Review\n\n- **Site Plan Sheets** - Transformer pad locations, dimensions, clearances to buildings and site features, property lines\n- **Electrical Plan Sheets** - Transformer locations, electrical routing to meters, transformer easements, service type specifications\n- **Utility Plan Sheets** - Underground utility locations, conflicts with transformer pads, utility crossings, overhead line locations\n- **Landscape Plan Sheets** - Tree species identification, vegetation proximity to transformer pads, root barrier details\n- **Detail Sheets** - Transformer pad construction details matching AE Design-approved specifications, bollard details, protective barrier specifications\n- **Easement Exhibits** - Transformer easements, drainage easements, utility easements, prohibited areas, property boundaries\n- **Building Elevation Sheets** - Vertical clearances from windows, balconies, overhangs, fire escape locations\n- **Cross-Section Drawings** - Vertical clearances from overhead structures, parking garages, enclosed areas, niche configurations showing 35-foot clearance\n- **Grading and Drainage Plans** - Surface drainage direction around transformer pads (for oil-filled equipment)\n- **Architectural Specifications** - Fire ratings for walls/structures in reduced clearance areas (3-hour rating verification)\n- **Austin Energy Design Approval Documents** - ESPA (Electric Service Planning Application), transformer pad detail sheets, service type approval, written approvals for clearance reductions or variances\n\n## Checklist Items\n\n| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n| EL-13.1 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from buildings, structures, and building foundations | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4; UCM § 1.10.5.B | All sites |\n| EL-13.2 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from retaining walls | Reviewer Convention | Only when retaining walls are present. Check: site plan and grading plan. |\n| EL-13.3 | Transformer pads lack required clearance from fences and gates - 5 feet for sides with operators/controls, 3 feet for sides without operators/controls (from removable ventilated fences) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when fences or gates are adjacent to transformer pads. Check: site plan showing fence locations and transformer access door orientation. |\n| EL-13.4 | Transformer pads with reduced clearances (3 feet to brick/masonry buildings) lack documentation of Austin Energy Design written approval or do not meet required conditions (not in confined space, 10 ft+ to windows/doors/ducts, 15 ft+ vertical clearance to windows/doors/ducts, 3-hour fire rating) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 2 | Only when clearances less than 5 feet to buildings are shown. Check: site plan dimensions and architectural specifications for fire rating. |\n| EL-13.5 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot lateral clearance from balconies and building overhangs, or have coverings/structures directly above pad or minimum equipment area | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when balconies or overhangs are present above or adjacent to transformer. Check: site plan, building elevations, and building cross-sections. |\n| EL-13.6 | Transformer pads lack minimum 12-foot horizontal clearance from windows, doors, or ventilating ducts when window/door/duct is less than 12 feet from grade or has less than 5 feet lateral separation (oil-filled equipment) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when windows, doors, or ventilating ducts are present on adjacent buildings with inadequate lateral separation or low grade elevation. Check: site plan, building elevations, and transformer equipment specifications. |\n| EL-13.7 | Transformer pads not located minimum 2 feet from back of sidewalks | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when sidewalks are adjacent to transformer pads. Check: site plan. |\n| EL-13.8 | Transformer pads lack required clearance from stairwells serving as fire exits | Reviewer Convention | Only when stairwells are present adjacent to transformer. Check: site plan and building floor plans. |\n| EL-13.9 | Transformer pads lack minimum 20-foot horizontal/radial clearance from fire escapes or stairs serving as fire escapes (oil-filled equipment) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when fire escapes or stairs serving as fire escapes are present. Check: site plan and building elevations showing fire escape locations. |\n| EL-13.10 | Transformer pad hot-stick use area (access door side) lacks minimum 10-foot clearance to open area or adjacent building/structure | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | All sites |\n| EL-13.11 | Transformer pad hot-stick use area (access door side) lacks minimum 15-foot clearance when facing confined space (niche, vault, or enclosed area on 3+ sides) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when transformer location faces confined space such as niche, vault, or notched-out building area. Check: site plan configuration and building cross-sections. |\n| EL-13.12 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from fire hydrants | Reviewer Convention | Only when fire hydrants are present. Check: site plan showing fire hydrant locations. |\n| EL-13.13 | Trees within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted equipment are not identified as utility-compatible species per ECM Appendix F, or trees within 15 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductors are not utility-compatible | UCM § 1.10.10.4; ECM Appendix F | Only when trees are proposed within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted equipment or 15 lateral feet of overhead distribution. Check: landscape plan species list cross-referenced with ECM Appendix F \"UC\" column. |\n| EL-13.14 | Shrubs or low-lying vegetation lack minimum 10 lateral feet clearance from transformer access door side, or lack minimum 5 lateral feet clearance from other sides (non-access door) | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when shrubs, vegetation, or landscaping is adjacent to transformer. Check: landscape plan showing planting locations relative to transformer access door orientation. |\n| EL-13.15 | Trees within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment lack required root barriers (minimum 4 feet deep, installed 5 feet from equipment) | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when trees are proposed within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment. Check: landscape plan and detail sheets for root barrier specifications. |\n| EL-13.16 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from dumpsters | Reviewer Convention | Only when dumpsters are present. Check: site plan showing dumpster locations and enclosures. |\n| EL-13.17 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from bike racks | Reviewer Convention | Only when bike racks are present. Check: site plan. |\n| EL-13.18 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from light poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when light poles are adjacent to transformer. Check: site plan and photometric plan. |\n| EL-13.19 | Transformer pads lack minimum 15-foot clearance from fire lanes | Reviewer Convention | Only when fire lanes are designated. Check: site plan showing fire lane striping, signage, and plan notes. |\n| EL-13.20 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from other transformer pads | Reviewer Convention | Only when multiple transformer pads are proposed. Check: electrical plan. |\n| EL-13.21 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot horizontal clearance from water lines | UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 | All sites |\n| EL-13.22 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot horizontal clearance from wastewater lines | UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 | All sites |\n| EL-13.23 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot horizontal clearance from storm drain lines | UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 | All sites |\n| EL-13.24 | Transformer pads lack minimum horizontal clearance from gas lines (24 inches minimum for gas pipelines less than 60 psi, 36 inches minimum for high-pressure gas 60 psi and over) | UCM § 1.10.5.A | Only when gas service is present. Check: utility plan showing gas infrastructure and pressure specifications. |\n| EL-13.25 | Transformer pads lack minimum 12-inch horizontal and vertical clearance from other underground utilities (except gas, fuel, steam) | UCM § 1.10.5.A | All sites |\n| EL-13.26 | Transformer pads or AE underground facilities located over or under other utility lines, or customer buildings/foundations/structures installed over AE underground facilities | UCM § 1.10.5.B | All sites |\n| EL-13.27 | Transformer locations lack minimum 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from overhead distribution line conductors (primary, neutral, and secondary), extending from ground to sky | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution lines are present. Check: site plan and utility plan showing overhead lines and proposed structures/features. |\n| EL-13.28 | Transformer locations or proposed facilities lack minimum 15-foot radius clearance from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors (measured from conductors, not pole centerline) | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution primary or neutral conductors are present. Check: utility plan showing overhead line locations and proposed site features. |\n| EL-13.29 | Transformer locations in enclosed or notched-out areas (niches) lack minimum 35-foot vertical clearance inside niche and niche entrance, or lack cross-section drawings demonstrating clearance | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 3 | Only when transformer is located in niche (accessible from outside but within building footprint), enclosed area, or notched-out building configuration. Check: site plan configuration, building cross-sections, and niche detail drawings. |\n| EL-13.30 | Niche service installations lack required 3-hour fire wall rating for walls and ceilings, proper ventilation specifications, or documentation of consultation with Austin Energy Design business unit | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 3 | Only when niche service is proposed (equipment within building footprint, accessible from outside). Check: architectural specifications and electrical plan notes for AE Design coordination. |\n| EL-13.31 | Cross-section drawings not provided showing vertical clearances in access paths with overhead structures, parking garages, or enclosed areas | UCM § 1.10.3 (survey requirement as required by AE Design) | Only when overhead structures, parking garages, enclosed areas, or niches are present above or adjacent to transformer locations. Check: plan set for cross-section sheets demonstrating vertical clearance compliance. |\n| EL-13.32 | Grading plan shows drainage slopes toward building from oil-filled transformer pad location (liquid flow must be away from building) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 1 | Only when oil-filled transformer equipment is proposed. Check: grading and drainage plans showing surface flow direction around transformer pad, and transformer equipment specifications. |\n| EL-13.33 | Transformer pads not located 100% on private property, located in public right-of-way, or straddling property lines | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 4 | All sites |\n| EL-13.34 | Transformer pads located in prohibited easements including drainage easements, water easements, sewer easements, or electrical easements under overhead lines without Austin Energy Design written approval | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 4 (property requirement); easement-specific regulations | All sites |\n| EL-13.35 | Transformer easements not shown around pads, or easement width inadequate for Austin Energy accessibility requirements | UCM § 1.10.3 (additional clearance and access easements may be required) | All sites |\n| EL-13.36 | Electrical routing from transformer to meter not 100% on private property | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 4 | All sites |\n| EL-13.37 | Transformer pad dimensions do not match Austin Energy Design-approved transformer pad detail sheets for specified service type (single-phase or three-phase) | UCM § 1.5.2.6; UCM § 1.18.0 (obtain transformer pad detail from AE Design) | All sites |\n| EL-13.38 | Equipment pad installed within 4 feet of parking areas or vehicle routes lacks required 4-inch minimum diameter galvanized rigid metal posts (bollards) per AE Design equipment pad details, or bollards not spaced to allow equipment door opening | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when transformer pad is located within 4 feet of parking areas, drive aisles, or vehicle circulation routes. Check: site plan showing parking layout, circulation, and bollard locations/specifications. |\n| EL-13.39 | Customer facilities (buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks) installed under or over Austin Energy overhead distribution facilities | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution facilities are present. Check: site plan and utility plan showing overhead line locations and proposed structures. |","numLines":142,"startLine":1,"totalLines":142}}}
