{"level":30,"time":1778188363162,"pid":122,"hostname":"e70bc6df-98e","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":6,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014oqi4cjjgTTMv82QfzUUmJ","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a visual analysis of the provided site plan document, there are notes addressing the 48-hour notification and safety requirements for working near overhead lines, though it does not explicitly cite \"Texas Health & Safety Code Chapter 752\" by name. It does, however, explicitly mandate a 48-hour notice to Austin Energy and references \"Texas State laws\" regarding overhead power line clearances. \n\nHere are the exact findings from the document:\n\n**1. 48-Hour Notification Requirement:**\nUnder the **Electric Transmission Notes** section (top middle), Note #3 specifically requires a 48-hour notice for a safety meeting prior to construction near transmission lines:\n> \"3. A pre-construction safety meeting is required with Austin Energy 48 hours before commencement of construction. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a tailgate safety meeting. Include Chuck Hendry (at 505-7151) in the meeting, if cranes are being used during construction. OSHA requires a 20' clearance from energized transmission lines during construction.\"\n\n**2. Reference to Laws Regarding Overhead Power Lines:**\nWhile \"Chapter 752\" is not explicitly printed, the requirement to adhere to Texas laws regarding proximity to overhead lines is present. Under the **Electric Notes** section (middle left), Note #4 states (this text is also repeated at the bottom of the **Electric Transmission Notes** section):\n> \"4. The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas State laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless required clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\""}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"426b854d-ee1b-489d-9b1e-65178de6c706","uuid":"9e9280c9-bd10-4b9d-a0e9-ad163717757e","tool_use_result":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a visual analysis of the provided site plan document, there are notes addressing the 48-hour notification and safety requirements for working near overhead lines, though it does not explicitly cite \"Texas Health & Safety Code Chapter 752\" by name. It does, however, explicitly mandate a 48-hour notice to Austin Energy and references \"Texas State laws\" regarding overhead power line clearances. \n\nHere are the exact findings from the document:\n\n**1. 48-Hour Notification Requirement:**\nUnder the **Electric Transmission Notes** section (top middle), Note #3 specifically requires a 48-hour notice for a safety meeting prior to construction near transmission lines:\n> \"3. A pre-construction safety meeting is required with Austin Energy 48 hours before commencement of construction. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a tailgate safety meeting. Include Chuck Hendry (at 505-7151) in the meeting, if cranes are being used during construction. OSHA requires a 20' clearance from energized transmission lines during construction.\"\n\n**2. Reference to Laws Regarding Overhead Power Lines:**\nWhile \"Chapter 752\" is not explicitly printed, the requirement to adhere to Texas laws regarding proximity to overhead lines is present. Under the **Electric Notes** section (middle left), Note #4 states (this text is also repeated at the bottom of the **Electric Transmission Notes** section):\n> \"4. The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas State laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless required clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\""}]}
