{"level":30,"time":1778188437954,"pid":122,"hostname":"e70bc6df-98e","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NCinXUR12svBEFaa1wXNR9","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a visual analysis of the provided site plan document:\n\n**Are overhead electric utility lines shown on this site plan?**\nYes, overhead electric utility lines are shown on the site plan.\n\n**What is their voltage classification or labeling (distribution vs. transmission)?**\nThe lines are labeled simply with the text \"**OHE**\" (a standard abbreviation for Overhead Electric) embedded within dashed line types (e.g., `---- OHE ----`). There is no explicit voltage classification (e.g., specific kV ratings) or text explicitly labeling them as \"distribution\" or \"transmission\" visible on this page. \n\n**What is the extent and location of these overhead lines relative to the proposed development?**\nThe overhead lines are located along the perimeter of the proposed development, specifically:\n1.  **Western Boundary:** An `OHE` line runs generally north-to-south along the western property boundary/frontage, roughly parallel to the adjacent roadway and sidewalk. Utility pole symbols (small circles) are periodically shown along this line.\n2.  **Southern Boundary:** The `OHE` line turns and continues along the entire length of the southern property boundary from the southwest corner to the southeast corner. \n\nThese lines remain on the outer edges of the property line and do not appear to cross through the interior of the site or over the proposed building structures (Bldgs 1-8)."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8e2cb63b-05d8-41aa-bedd-77a52405a611","uuid":"a6c2395b-644c-464e-80cd-3fc4645e5c1e","tool_use_result":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a visual analysis of the provided site plan document:\n\n**Are overhead electric utility lines shown on this site plan?**\nYes, overhead electric utility lines are shown on the site plan.\n\n**What is their voltage classification or labeling (distribution vs. transmission)?**\nThe lines are labeled simply with the text \"**OHE**\" (a standard abbreviation for Overhead Electric) embedded within dashed line types (e.g., `---- OHE ----`). There is no explicit voltage classification (e.g., specific kV ratings) or text explicitly labeling them as \"distribution\" or \"transmission\" visible on this page. \n\n**What is the extent and location of these overhead lines relative to the proposed development?**\nThe overhead lines are located along the perimeter of the proposed development, specifically:\n1.  **Western Boundary:** An `OHE` line runs generally north-to-south along the western property boundary/frontage, roughly parallel to the adjacent roadway and sidewalk. Utility pole symbols (small circles) are periodically shown along this line.\n2.  **Southern Boundary:** The `OHE` line turns and continues along the entire length of the southern property boundary from the southwest corner to the southeast corner. \n\nThese lines remain on the outer edges of the property line and do not appear to cross through the interior of the site or over the proposed building structures (Bldgs 1-8)."}]}
